BASENAME(1)                    P32W Unix                     BASENAME(1)

NAME
       basename - strip directory and suffix from a file name

SYNOPSIS
       basename string [suffix]

DESCRIPTION
       basename prints string with any trailing slashes and then
       everything up to and including its last / removed. A string
       of slashes only gives /. If suffix is given and the rest ends
       with it (and is longer than it), the suffix is removed too.
       It is used in shell scripts, inside back quotes.

EXAMPLES
       basename /usr/ken/prog.c      prints prog.c
       basename /usr/ken/prog.c .c   prints prog
       basename /usr/ken/            prints ken
       cc -o `basename $f .c` $f

EXIT STATUS
       0; 2 for a usage error (no argument, or more than two).

SEE ALSO
       dirname(1), sh(1), expr(1)
